1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,000
They operate perfectly for years on end in the harsh environment of outer space.

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Strong enough to survive their fiery launch into orbit.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:17,000
Sophisticated enough to provide life-saving images or relay tens of thousands of phone calls at the same time.

4
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:27,000
By monitoring weapon systems and troop movements, these eyes in the sky may be the difference between security and annihilation.

5
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:35,000
An American physicist tinkering in his aunt's field, a visionary British science fiction writer,

6
00:00:35,001 --> 00:00:39,000
a German rocket designer for the Nazi war machine,

7
00:00:39,001 --> 00:00:46,000
and an unparalleled technological race between superpowers all hastened their arrival.

8
00:00:46,001 --> 00:00:51,000
For four decades they have been the most precious cargo on Earth,

9
00:00:51,001 --> 00:00:57,000
Hurtling skyward only after grand failures gave way to grander triumphs.

10
00:00:57,001 --> 00:01:03,000
Coming up, from the crude early models to some of the most precise and durable machines ever built,

11
00:01:03,001 --> 00:01:06,000
satellites linking our world.

12
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Silently, more than 500 of them orbit our Earth.

13
00:01:37,280 --> 00:01:41,040
It's hard to imagine a world where we don't have satellites.

14
00:01:41,980 --> 00:01:47,540
It has literally changed the way in which we live since the mid-1960s.

15
00:01:47,541 --> 00:01:53,540
Today's high-tech satellites are some of the most complex machines ever invented.

16
00:01:53,541 --> 00:02:00,540
They are the crucial link in an ever-increasing number of tasks such as weather forecasting,

17
00:02:00,541 --> 00:02:08,540
global telephone, fax and television communication, military and commercial reconnaissance, and even navigation.

18
00:02:08,541 --> 00:02:15,540
But just over 50 years ago, satellites were simply an idea in the mind of a British scientist

19
00:02:15,541 --> 00:02:19,540
famous for his science fiction writing.

20
00:02:19,541 --> 00:02:20,241
Arthur C.

21
00:02:20,242 --> 00:02:25,540
Clarke was the first man to envision many of the modern applications of satellites.

22
00:02:25,541 --> 00:02:29,540
He also penned 2001, A Space Odyssey.

23
00:02:29,541 --> 00:02:33,540
And in 1945, 12 years before Sputnik's launch,

24
00:02:33,541 --> 00:02:36,540
he wrote an article in Wireless World magazine

25
00:02:36,541 --> 00:02:40,540
describing how satellites could be placed in geostationary orbit

26
00:02:40,541 --> 00:02:44,540
and used to relay signals.

27
00:02:45,540 --> 00:02:49,380
Today, most communication satellites do operate in this orbit,

28
00:02:49,760 --> 00:02:56,340
rotating at the same rate as the Earth because of their location, 22,300 miles above the equator.

29
00:02:57,620 --> 00:03:01,540
In addition, Clark foresaw polar-orbiting weather satellites

30
00:03:01,541 --> 00:03:08,080
and advocated an orbiting space telescope, which today exists as NASA's Hubble telescope.

31
00:03:08,081 --> 00:03:16,660
So if any person had the most ideas at the earliest time about the modern applications

32
00:03:16,661 --> 00:03:21,960
of satellites, Arthur Clarke was the one.

33
00:03:21,961 --> 00:03:26,580
Arthur Clarke was one of a handful of people at that time who had confidence that man-made

34
00:03:26,581 --> 00:03:29,320
objects would one day circle the planet.

35
00:03:38,080 --> 00:03:43,580
Satellites have to be propelled at very high speeds in order to avoid being pulled back to Earth.

36
00:03:44,080 --> 00:03:51,440
For example, the faster a bullet is fired from a rifle, the further it travels before being pulled back to Earth by gravity.

37
00:03:52,440 --> 00:04:00,880
But theoretically, if a bullet could be fired at 17,500 miles an hour, it would circle the planet as a satellite.

38
00:04:00,881 --> 00:04:09,760
Today, only rockets can propel something at this very high speed, equal to about 5 miles

39
00:04:09,761 --> 00:04:11,760
per second.

40
00:04:11,761 --> 00:04:17,400
You have to have a propulsion unit which will take the thing up and put it into a flight

41
00:04:17,401 --> 00:04:21,380
path generally parallel to the Earth.

42
00:04:21,381 --> 00:04:25,620
Now of course, gravity is going to try to pull that down.

43
00:04:25,621 --> 00:04:31,280
And if you put this at high enough speed, you generate enough centrifugal force that

44
00:04:31,281 --> 00:04:36,120
it counteracts the force of gravity trying to pull it down.

45
00:04:36,121 --> 00:04:41,900
In other words, the amount that the satellite is pulled closer to Earth by gravity is canceled

46
00:04:41,901 --> 00:04:45,820
by the curvature of the Earth away from the speeding spacecraft.

47
00:04:45,821 --> 00:04:51,720
It continually falls around the Earth instead of falling down to the surface.

48
00:04:51,721 --> 00:04:57,560
This is the principle that guided Arthur C. Clarke's reasoning.

49
00:04:57,561 --> 00:05:02,480
Once a satellite is released from the main rocket, small booster rockets adjust the satellite's

50
00:05:02,481 --> 00:05:07,100
orientation to point it towards the correct spot on Earth.

51
00:05:07,101 --> 00:05:12,180
And since there is no atmosphere in space to slow the orbiter down, the rockets only

52
00:05:12,181 --> 00:05:17,880
need to fire if the satellite slightly deviates from its charted course.

53
00:05:17,881 --> 00:05:24,880
However, just because the theory behind turning an object into an Earth satellite seems simple

54
00:05:24,881 --> 00:05:30,880
does not mean we can launch anything we want into orbit.

55
00:05:30,881 --> 00:05:33,880
Weight and cost have always been concerns.

56
00:05:33,881 --> 00:05:36,880
Some of the first satellites weighed only a few pounds,

57
00:05:36,881 --> 00:05:40,880
but still cost millions of dollars to build and send into space.

58
00:05:40,881 --> 00:05:45,880
Today, thanks to more powerful rockets, satellites can weigh more than a ton,

59
00:05:45,881 --> 00:05:52,720
a ton, but launching is still not cheap at $30,000 per pound.

60
00:05:52,721 --> 00:05:57,900
The ultimate extensions of size and weight in satellite technology are the manned space

61
00:05:57,901 --> 00:06:06,560
stations where astronauts can live for months at a time while circling the planet.

62
00:06:06,561 --> 00:06:11,720
Currently several countries, including the United States and Russia, are working together

63
00:06:11,721 --> 00:06:15,040
to build a new international space station.

64
00:06:15,041 --> 00:06:20,040
Yet for much of the world's history in space, cooperation was the furthest thing from the

65
00:06:20,041 --> 00:06:23,920
minds of the leaders of these two superpowers.

66
00:06:23,921 --> 00:06:37,560
Prosperity and national confidence were running rampant in 1950s America.

67
00:06:37,561 --> 00:06:42,240
Dwight Eisenhower, the leader of the Allied Army that won World War II, was in the White

68
00:06:42,241 --> 00:06:43,640
House.

69
00:06:43,641 --> 00:06:49,460
Tail fins, malt shops, and Elvis Presley symbolized the enthusiasm of a new era.

70
00:06:50,960 --> 00:06:54,600
American consumers reveled in unparalleled materialism.

71
00:06:56,220 --> 00:07:02,300
The Soviet Union was a concern, but America had been first to develop the atom bomb,

72
00:07:02,301 --> 00:07:07,420
and most citizens assumed that the U.S. was still the world's technological leader.

73
00:07:07,421 --> 00:07:16,900
until October 4, 1957, when the Soviet Union launched something they called Sputnik, meaning traveling companion.

74
00:07:19,680 --> 00:07:23,020
My own personal reaction was, gee, that's great.

75
00:07:23,860 --> 00:07:29,360
But the general public, of course, raised a hue and cry that we somehow let the Russians get ahead.

76
00:07:30,380 --> 00:07:35,260
The successful operation of Sputnik as the world's first artificial satellite

77
00:07:35,261 --> 00:07:38,880
dealt a sledgehammer blow to America's national pride.

78
00:07:39,960 --> 00:07:46,400
It was simply a 184-pound test payload containing a radio beacon and a thermometer.

79
00:07:48,100 --> 00:07:53,860
The beacon was used to prove that artificial objects in outer space could be tracked from Earth.

80
00:07:55,440 --> 00:08:00,020
But even though the technology was fairly simple, its impact was enormous.

81
00:08:00,860 --> 00:08:06,300
Many people worried that if the Soviet Union had the rocket technology to launch something into space,

82
00:08:06,820 --> 00:08:11,620
there was nothing stopping them from launching nuclear warheads capable of reaching America,

83
00:08:12,240 --> 00:08:15,020
an attack which the U.S. could not counter.

84
00:08:16,620 --> 00:08:23,820
Yet, for all the fear that Sputnik raised, there were actually several positive benefits for the United States.

85
00:08:23,821 --> 00:08:31,860
The Soviets really did us a very big favor by launching Sputnik first, because they eliminated

86
00:08:31,861 --> 00:08:37,920
any legal challenge to overflight of territory with a satellite, because they essentially

87
00:08:37,921 --> 00:08:41,500
did it themselves and so they would have a hard time complaining about anything that

88
00:08:41,501 --> 00:08:44,500
we did after that.

89
00:08:44,501 --> 00:08:48,552
Millions of dollars began to pour into the coffers of the fledgling U.S.

90
00:08:48,553 --> 00:08:49,340
space program.

91
00:08:49,341 --> 00:08:53,540
Clearly, America did not enjoy being second best to anyone.

92
00:08:54,360 --> 00:08:56,200
The space race was on.

93
00:09:05,860 --> 00:09:08,840
At the time of the successful Sputnik launch,

94
00:09:09,100 --> 00:09:11,740
the U.S. had already been working for several years

95
00:09:11,741 --> 00:09:14,020
on the development of its own satellites.

96
00:09:15,160 --> 00:09:17,140
Initially, many people in the government

97
00:09:17,141 --> 00:09:19,300
weren't convinced about their usefulness.

98
00:09:19,860 --> 00:09:22,100
In fact, before Sputnik was launched,

99
00:09:22,600 --> 00:09:24,880
American proponents of space operations

100
00:09:24,881 --> 00:09:27,600
were still struggling for respect.

101
00:09:28,940 --> 00:09:33,160
We were considered, frankly, a bunch of crackpots and nuts.

102
00:09:33,980 --> 00:09:35,800
One time after I'd given a lecture

103
00:09:35,801 --> 00:09:37,780
and had been quoted in a newspaper

104
00:09:37,781 --> 00:09:40,920
about military use of satellites for reconnaissance

105
00:09:40,921 --> 00:09:42,080
and how you could counter them,

106
00:09:42,700 --> 00:09:46,360
this very well-meaning senior man came up to me and said,

107
00:09:46,361 --> 00:09:51,140
you've got to stop talking like that because you are destroying your reputation.

108
00:09:51,820 --> 00:09:54,760
Sputnik changed that all overnight.

109
00:09:56,700 --> 00:10:00,320
Surprisingly, we can trace the roots of the American space program

110
00:10:00,321 --> 00:10:03,300
back to a cabbage patch in Auburn, Massachusetts.

111
00:10:04,520 --> 00:10:09,220
On March 16, 1926, physicist Robert Goddard

112
00:10:09,221 --> 00:10:12,820
launched the first liquid-propelled rocket in the world.

113
00:10:12,821 --> 00:10:20,980
The liquid oxygen and gasoline-powered cylinder rose a modest 41 feet in the air before returning

114
00:10:20,981 --> 00:10:22,580
to his Aunt Effie's field.

115
00:10:22,581 --> 00:10:27,148
His flight in 1926 was a path breaker worldwide.

116
00:10:27,149 --> 00:10:31,240
Lots of people had understood solid rockets

117
00:10:31,241 --> 00:10:35,020
for a long time, Roman candles and things of that nature that we've been familiar with

118
00:10:35,021 --> 00:10:36,077
for centuries.

119
00:10:36,078 --> 00:10:41,440
But in order to really do space flight, liquid propellants are the key.

120
00:10:41,441 --> 00:10:49,800
Only liquid propellants can be throttled, providing different amounts of thrust at different stages of a rocket's flight.

121
00:10:52,160 --> 00:10:57,160
Goddard continued his experiments in Roswell, New Mexico during the 30s and early 40s.

122
00:10:57,980 --> 00:11:04,000
Eventually, his rockets achieved altitudes of one mile, but his vision went much further.

123
00:11:04,640 --> 00:11:10,580
In his early days, Goddard had told a member of the press that a rocket could be built to reach the moon.

124
00:11:11,300 --> 00:11:17,540
He was ridiculed in scientific circles and by the man on the street for such a preposterous idea.

125
00:11:19,060 --> 00:11:26,600
The wide open spaces of New Mexico provided a haven where he could continue to work on his dream, away from the public spotlight.

126
00:11:27,800 --> 00:11:35,220
However, because of his death in 1945, he never witnessed an object being launched into outer space.

127
00:11:35,221 --> 00:11:42,120
The next major contribution to the American space program

128
00:11:42,121 --> 00:11:46,000
came from a very unlikely place, Nazi Germany.

129
00:11:46,960 --> 00:11:53,000
Lead scientist Werner von Braun and about 120 other German scientists and engineers

130
00:11:53,001 --> 00:11:57,000
fled from their rocket testing facility in Piena Munde, Germany

131
00:11:57,001 --> 00:12:00,800
and were escorted from Austria back to the United States

132
00:12:00,801 --> 00:12:04,400
in the final days of World War II by U.S. Army personnel.

133
00:12:05,220 --> 00:12:12,460
Von Braun had been fascinated by rockets from an early age.

134
00:12:12,960 --> 00:12:17,560
Now he could use his knowledge to explore space rather than delivering bombs.

135
00:12:19,300 --> 00:12:24,040
In fact, during World War II, von Braun had been jailed by the Gestapo

136
00:12:24,041 --> 00:12:27,980
for making statements about his desire to work on space travel projects.

137
00:12:28,860 --> 00:12:33,980
For the Nazis, anything less than 100% devotion to the V-2 superweapon

138
00:12:33,981 --> 00:12:36,620
was seen as malicious sabotage.

139
00:12:37,400 --> 00:12:41,280
However, since von Braun was so important to the German war effort,

140
00:12:41,700 --> 00:12:44,140
he remained in jail for only two weeks.

141
00:12:46,020 --> 00:12:50,580
The U.S. brought back several of the 29,000-pound V-2 rockets

142
00:12:50,581 --> 00:12:54,460
that Hitler's army had been using to terrorize Europe during the war.

143
00:12:58,160 --> 00:13:02,160
Soon, these former enemies were using their one-time secret weapons

144
00:13:02,161 --> 00:13:05,900
to conduct groundbreaking experiments for the American military.

145
00:13:07,820 --> 00:13:12,500
However, the propulsion unit initially chosen to power the first U.S. satellite,

146
00:13:12,960 --> 00:13:16,620
the three-pound Vanguard I, was supplied by the Navy

147
00:13:16,621 --> 00:13:19,620
instead of von Braun's team of Army scientists.

148
00:13:21,220 --> 00:13:25,720
On December 6, 1957, the Navy got their chance.

149
00:13:25,721 --> 00:13:34,060
Dozens of Newsreel cameras and hundreds of reporters

150
00:13:34,061 --> 00:13:38,060
witnessed the United States' first attempt to catch up in the space race

151
00:13:38,061 --> 00:13:39,740
go up in flames

152
00:13:39,741 --> 00:13:46,680
The government gave Werner von Braun a chance to redeem the sagging space program

153
00:13:46,681 --> 00:13:50,440
and von Braun gave Gordon Vaith a sneak peek at the rocket

154
00:13:50,441 --> 00:13:52,020
that would make it possible

155
00:13:52,021 --> 00:13:57,220
Von Braun took me one day down into a shed down in Huntsville, Alabama,

156
00:13:57,221 --> 00:14:02,120
at the Army Ballistic Missile Agency, and he showed me a rocket assembly.

157
00:14:02,540 --> 00:14:03,800
He said, there it is.

158
00:14:04,620 --> 00:14:11,540
When the government says we need a rocket assembly to launch a satellite, that's it.

159
00:14:11,880 --> 00:14:12,900
We're ready to go.

160
00:14:14,720 --> 00:14:18,300
Von Braun watched on January 31st, 1958,

161
00:14:18,680 --> 00:14:21,780
as the Army tried to launch the first American satellite.

162
00:14:22,020 --> 00:14:29,020
Explorer 1 nearly four months after Sputnik.

163
00:14:34,140 --> 00:14:37,780
The 31-pound Explorer 1 successfully entered orbit

164
00:14:37,781 --> 00:14:41,780
and remained in space eight years longer than Sputnik.

165
00:14:41,781 --> 00:14:46,080
After the launch, von Braun declared,

166
00:14:46,081 --> 00:14:49,260
we have firmly established our foothold in space.

167
00:14:49,261 --> 00:14:53,260
We will never give it up again.

168
00:14:53,261 --> 00:14:59,260
America launched dozens of other scientific explorer satellites over the next 17 years,

169
00:14:59,261 --> 00:15:05,260
most of them under the supervision of the government's newly formed space agency, NASA.

170
00:15:05,261 --> 00:15:15,260
Just eight months after Explorer 1, the National Aeronautics and Space Administration came into being on October 1, 1958.

171
00:15:15,261 --> 00:15:20,260
Its official purpose was the peaceful exploration of space.

172
00:15:20,261 --> 00:15:30,260
However, its practical goal during this stage of the Cold War was to catch up to and surpass the Soviet Union in the space race.

173
00:15:30,261 --> 00:15:34,260
But in 1961, the Soviets upped the ante.

174
00:15:34,261 --> 00:15:44,260
On April 12th, the Soviet Union launched a very different type of satellite, one with a man inside.

175
00:15:44,261 --> 00:15:50,140
Yuri Gagarin became the first human being in space and the first person to orbit the Earth.

176
00:15:50,960 --> 00:15:58,720
After reaching a maximum height of 203 miles, he landed in a field in the Soviet Union an hour and 48 minutes later,

177
00:15:58,721 --> 00:16:03,360
giving the communists another worldwide propaganda victory.

178
00:16:06,280 --> 00:16:09,500
After the historic flight, Gagarin said,

179
00:16:09,501 --> 00:16:17,880
I certainly did not feel lonely at all, for I knew that my friends, in fact all the Soviet people, were watching my spaceflight.

180
00:16:21,140 --> 00:16:25,960
Ten months later, John Glenn became America's first human satellite.

181
00:16:25,961 --> 00:16:29,080
You're underway. Godspeed, John Glenn.

182
00:16:30,820 --> 00:16:38,940
On February 20th, 1962, he circled the Earth three times at 17,500 miles an hour.

183
00:16:38,941 --> 00:16:42,720
Roger, zero G, and I feel fine. Capsule is turning around.

184
00:16:43,300 --> 00:16:44,940
Oh, that view is tremendous.

185
00:16:46,580 --> 00:16:52,200
Several days later, Glenn received a hero's welcome during a New York City ticker tape parade.

186
00:16:53,500 --> 00:16:59,200
This has been a great experience for all of us on the program and for all Americans, I guess, too.

187
00:17:00,600 --> 00:17:06,540
And I'm certainly glad to see that pride in our country and its accomplishments are not a thing of the past.

188
00:17:06,541 --> 00:17:11,160
But the United States had finished in second place again,

189
00:17:11,161 --> 00:17:15,060
and many people wondered if the country could really meet President John F.

190
00:17:15,061 --> 00:17:16,100
Kennedy's challenge.

191
00:17:17,580 --> 00:17:22,160
I believe that this nation should commit itself to achieving the goal,

192
00:17:22,880 --> 00:17:28,640
before this decade is out, of landing a man on the moon and returning him safely to the Earth.

193
00:17:29,820 --> 00:17:33,680
Billions of dollars continue to pour into the U.S. space program.

194
00:17:33,681 --> 00:17:47,680
Finally, on July 20, 1969, with the help of a 363-foot-tall, 6.3 million-pound Saturn V rocket, America finished first.

195
00:17:47,681 --> 00:17:54,680
first.

196
00:18:00,920 --> 00:18:05,000
The US had won the grandest public prize in the space race,

197
00:18:05,001 --> 00:18:07,960
and national confidence had been restored.

198
00:18:07,961 --> 00:18:12,340
But an even more crucial race continued.

199
00:18:12,341 --> 00:18:15,540
Reconnaissance satellites assumed critical importance

200
00:18:15,541 --> 00:18:19,900
as the superpowers continued to build their nuclear arsenals.

201
00:18:19,901 --> 00:18:25,660
If one side could pinpoint the locations of the other's missiles using satellite photos,

202
00:18:25,661 --> 00:18:30,120
then a lethal first strike would be possible.

203
00:18:30,121 --> 00:18:32,920
Effective reconnaissance equal preparedness,

204
00:18:32,921 --> 00:18:37,160
and a lack of preparedness could result in complete annihilation.

205
00:18:45,540 --> 00:18:54,540
While NASA conducted its highly publicized missions, the darker, covert side of the space race continued.

206
00:18:54,541 --> 00:19:03,540
The American military was working in great secrecy to design reconnaissance satellites to spy on their communist rival.

207
00:19:03,541 --> 00:19:14,019
Determining the number of Soviet missiles and the strength and location of its troops was vital information that could prevent the Cold War from spiraling out of

208
00:19:14,020 --> 00:19:14,720
control.

209
00:19:14,721 --> 00:19:15,421
control.

210
00:19:15,422 --> 00:19:21,540
But developing the technology to take detailed photographs from space was proving

211
00:19:21,541 --> 00:19:25,297
to be an extremely difficult challenge.

212
00:19:25,298 --> 00:19:29,920
The first attempt was the 1,300 pound Discoverer

213
00:19:29,921 --> 00:19:36,485
One, finally launched on February 28, 1959, after many delays.

214
00:19:36,486 --> 00:19:38,920
The government told the

215
00:19:38,921 --> 00:19:45,720
it was a scientific orbiter, but Discoverer was simply a cover name for the CIA's spy satellite

216
00:19:45,721 --> 00:19:48,220
project, code named Corona.

217
00:19:50,160 --> 00:19:55,640
Discoverer 1 did not carry a military payload, but it was intended to eventually prove out

218
00:19:55,641 --> 00:19:56,759
the technology.

219
00:19:56,760 --> 00:20:01,980
It ended up flying into the snow in Antarctica, and we then went on to

220
00:20:01,981 --> 00:20:05,921
Discoverer 2, which failed, and 3, which failed, and 4, which failed.

221
00:20:05,922 --> 00:20:07,120
And we had continuous

222
00:20:07,121 --> 00:20:13,740
failures with these early military satellites until Discoverer 13.

223
00:20:13,741 --> 00:20:20,720
On August 11, 1960, Discoverer 13 ejected a capsule covered by a heat shield which plummeted

224
00:20:20,721 --> 00:20:22,640
towards Earth.

225
00:20:22,641 --> 00:20:27,500
A Navy helicopter recovered it from the Pacific Ocean and quickly flew it to Washington,

226
00:20:27,501 --> 00:20:33,240
D.C., where the capsule's prize was presented to President Eisenhower, an American flag.

227
00:20:37,120 --> 00:20:43,860
Although Discoverer 13 had proven that an object could be brought back from outer space,

228
00:20:43,861 --> 00:20:49,800
top-secret photographs were not recovered until an Air Force plane literally plucked the parachute

229
00:20:49,801 --> 00:20:56,320
attached to Discoverer 14's film capsule out of the sky on August 19, 1960.

230
00:20:59,300 --> 00:21:04,840
It returned film of the Soviet Union, and it excited a lot of people within the intelligence community

231
00:21:04,841 --> 00:21:07,380
that the day of satellite reconnaissance was here.

232
00:21:08,580 --> 00:21:13,800
Although the CIA won't release altitude figures for modern reconnaissance orbiters,

233
00:21:14,180 --> 00:21:20,240
we do know that the Discoverer spy satellites operated in a low-Earth elliptical orbit,

234
00:21:20,680 --> 00:21:24,160
generally between 100 and 400 miles above the Earth.

235
00:21:24,780 --> 00:21:29,520
They flew over the poles in order to take pictures of many different locations

236
00:21:29,521 --> 00:21:33,420
as the world rotated underneath their polar path.

237
00:21:34,840 --> 00:21:40,840
However, the lag time between when a picture was taken by these early discoverer satellites

238
00:21:40,841 --> 00:21:45,940
and when a CIA analyst could look at the finished photo after the film was recovered,

239
00:21:46,280 --> 00:21:50,280
sent back to Washington, D.C., and developed was about two weeks.

240
00:21:50,760 --> 00:21:52,320
Hardly instant analysis.

241
00:21:53,380 --> 00:21:57,620
But technicians hoped that the new satellite images would at least be an improvement

242
00:21:57,621 --> 00:22:00,860
over existing U-2 spy plane photos.

243
00:22:00,861 --> 00:22:09,180
Photo interpreters who were using the U-2 imagery were told to put that imagery aside

244
00:22:09,181 --> 00:22:14,700
and when they got the new film from the satellites, they looked at it and they were rather disappointed.

245
00:22:14,840 --> 00:22:17,080
It was not as good a resolution as they expected.

246
00:22:17,720 --> 00:22:21,320
And so they actually went back and pulled out their U-2 photography again.

247
00:22:22,640 --> 00:22:27,540
But two events in the early 1960s convinced experts that the long-term success

248
00:22:27,541 --> 00:22:31,240
of the military satellite reconnaissance program was crucial.

249
00:22:33,420 --> 00:22:40,980
On May 1, 1960, Soviet surface-to-air missiles shot down Air Force pilot Francis Gary Powers'

250
00:22:41,140 --> 00:22:45,060
U-2 spy plane 80,000 feet above the Soviet Union.

251
00:22:48,020 --> 00:22:53,180
Powers survived, but in October 1962, during the Cuban Missile Crisis,

252
00:22:53,740 --> 00:22:56,520
the Soviets shot down another U-2 airplane.

253
00:22:56,521 --> 00:22:58,920
and this time the pilot was killed.

254
00:23:00,120 --> 00:23:05,220
It was obvious that better satellite images would allow vital information to be gathered

255
00:23:05,221 --> 00:23:08,040
without risking more American lives.

256
00:23:09,780 --> 00:23:13,980
An indication of America's commitment to space-based surveillance

257
00:23:13,981 --> 00:23:19,500
was the establishment in August 1960 of the National Reconnaissance Office.

258
00:23:20,700 --> 00:23:23,500
Its very existence was classified,

259
00:23:23,501 --> 00:23:29,860
and its job was to spy on the Soviet Union as well as China, Libya and other countries.

260
00:23:31,420 --> 00:23:37,200
In 1962, all of the individual reconnaissance satellite projects in the NRO

261
00:23:37,201 --> 00:23:40,460
fell under the general codename of Keyhole.

262
00:23:42,660 --> 00:23:46,060
Following the failures to recover many of the film capsules

263
00:23:46,061 --> 00:23:51,080
and the two-week lag time between taking and developing discoverer satellite pictures,

264
00:23:51,081 --> 00:23:55,420
the NRO struggled to improve their eyes in the sky.

265
00:23:57,120 --> 00:24:00,080
During the 1960s and early 1970s,

266
00:24:00,980 --> 00:24:04,460
more efficient satellites containing several film capsules

267
00:24:04,461 --> 00:24:08,360
and a more streamlined development and analysis process

268
00:24:08,361 --> 00:24:12,000
reduced the delay time to three or four days.

269
00:24:12,640 --> 00:24:17,160
But film was still physically sent back from space in canisters

270
00:24:17,161 --> 00:24:22,460
because several experiments to transmit images using radio signals had failed.

271
00:24:23,840 --> 00:24:28,840
Two world events proved that a several-day delay was still too long.

272
00:24:32,460 --> 00:24:37,900
On August 21, 1968, Soviet troops invaded Czechoslovakia

273
00:24:37,901 --> 00:24:41,800
to suppress a pro-democracy movement centered in the capital of Prague.

274
00:24:42,480 --> 00:24:48,920
And on October 6, 1973, Egypt and Syria launched a surprise attack on Israel.

275
00:24:53,020 --> 00:25:00,460
In both cases, keyhole satellites did not return photographs until several days after the invasions had occurred.

276
00:25:01,200 --> 00:25:05,460
As one CIA analyst said of the 1973 battle in the Middle East,

277
00:25:06,080 --> 00:25:10,960
we had wonderful coverage, but we didn't get the photographs until the war was over.

278
00:25:11,800 --> 00:25:21,000
I, Jimmy Carter, do solemnly swear.

279
00:25:21,700 --> 00:25:23,480
I, Jimmy Carter, do solemnly swear.

280
00:25:23,481 --> 00:25:35,780
On January 20, 1977, a brisk winter day, Jimmy Carter walked down Pennsylvania Avenue following his inauguration as President of the United States.

281
00:25:35,781 --> 00:25:42,780
However, another exciting event was also taking place just a few miles away.

282
00:25:42,781 --> 00:25:54,515
That same afternoon, the newest model of Keyhole Satellite was able to transmit the first nearly real-time images from outer space to the NRO Processing Center at Fort

283
00:25:54,516 --> 00:25:55,780
Belvoir, Virginia.

284
00:25:55,781 --> 00:26:04,780
Each dot of the photo, or pixel, was assigned a number corresponding to white, black, or varying shades of gray.

285
00:26:04,781 --> 00:26:11,460
These numbers were converted into a signal and beamed to Earth, where the digital transmission

286
00:26:11,461 --> 00:26:16,320
was converted back into a photo.

287
00:26:16,321 --> 00:26:21,020
President Carter did not see the images until the next day, but he immediately recognized

288
00:26:21,021 --> 00:26:26,460
their value in making critical decisions based on photographic facts instead of the theories

289
00:26:26,461 --> 00:26:28,160
of analysts.

290
00:26:28,161 --> 00:26:34,440
Keyhole Model 11 had just reduced the lag time for spy satellites from several days

291
00:26:34,441 --> 00:26:37,140
to a matter of minutes.

292
00:26:37,141 --> 00:26:41,080
But very few people in the government were allowed to see the top secret images from

293
00:26:41,081 --> 00:26:46,220
this new keyhole satellite, also known as KH-11.

294
00:26:46,221 --> 00:26:53,500
Even today, only satellite photos taken before 1972 have been declassified and made available

295
00:26:53,501 --> 00:26:55,740
to the public.

296
00:26:55,741 --> 00:27:00,460
However, this secret information has been prone to leaks over the years.

297
00:27:00,461 --> 00:27:08,320
In 1978, William Campiles, a former CIA employee, sold the entire KH-11 manual to the Soviets

298
00:27:08,321 --> 00:27:10,740
for $3,000.

299
00:27:10,741 --> 00:27:14,140
He received a 40-year prison sentence.

300
00:27:14,141 --> 00:27:16,267
Then, in 1984, a U.S.

301
00:27:16,268 --> 00:27:23,660
Naval Intelligence analyst leaked this KH-11 image to an English military

302
00:27:23,661 --> 00:27:25,140
magazine.

303
00:27:25,141 --> 00:27:31,920
It shows the first nuclear-powered Soviet aircraft carrier during construction.

304
00:27:31,921 --> 00:27:41,580
Samuel Long Morrison received a two-year sentence for leaking the photo.

305
00:27:41,581 --> 00:27:47,520
Since all modern information and images are still classified, the SPOT commercial photographic

306
00:27:47,521 --> 00:27:52,820
satellite developed in France gives us some idea of military satellite reconnaissance

307
00:27:52,821 --> 00:27:54,180
capabilities.

308
00:27:54,181 --> 00:28:00,100
It is used for a variety of business purposes, including urban planning, crop yield assessments,

309
00:28:00,101 --> 00:28:04,140
and environmental issues such as gauging deforestation.

310
00:28:04,141 --> 00:28:09,780
Still the Spot satellite is not nearly as accurate as military systems.

311
00:28:09,781 --> 00:28:16,440
Spot's resolution capacity is 33 feet, whereas experts believe today's military models may

312
00:28:16,441 --> 00:28:20,560
have resolutions of only a few inches.

313
00:28:20,561 --> 00:28:26,000
But very few people in the government know the exact resolution numbers.

314
00:28:26,001 --> 00:28:30,740
When you measure resolution, you are actually not measuring the smallest object you are

315
00:28:30,741 --> 00:28:32,080
seeing.

316
00:28:32,081 --> 00:28:37,180
You measure the smallest distance that you can determine between two objects.

317
00:28:37,181 --> 00:28:41,900
You cannot read license plates from a satellite in orbit.

318
00:28:41,901 --> 00:28:47,900
They have a resolution that is probably about the equivalent of a softball, about six inches.

319
00:28:47,901 --> 00:28:52,140
If two objects are about a softball apart, you can tell that from orbit.

320
00:28:53,940 --> 00:28:58,340
New reconnaissance systems even use infrared and ultraviolet sources

321
00:28:58,341 --> 00:29:01,340
instead of relying solely on the visible light spectrum.

322
00:29:02,380 --> 00:29:08,820
This means that these satellites can still obtain images on cloudy days or even at night.

323
00:29:10,220 --> 00:29:14,780
But the research and development devoted to American military satellites

324
00:29:14,781 --> 00:29:18,780
has also spawned a new wave of high-tech civilian uses.

325
00:29:19,680 --> 00:29:25,720
In fact, one type of satellite routinely saves thousands of lives every year.

326
00:29:28,720 --> 00:29:34,160
There is another type of orbiter whose origins are based in military space technology

327
00:29:34,161 --> 00:29:37,800
that provides a most critical civilian service.

328
00:29:44,780 --> 00:29:51,420
Some scientists believe that global warming will result in more frequent and more powerful

329
00:29:51,421 --> 00:29:54,760
natural disasters in the coming years.

330
00:29:54,761 --> 00:30:01,280
In the past, commercial and residential property losses due to tornadoes, floods and hurricanes

331
00:30:01,281 --> 00:30:07,440
have often escalated into the hundreds of millions and sometimes billions of dollars.

332
00:30:07,441 --> 00:30:13,900
Yet there is one figure that has been decreasing worldwide over the last few decades, fatalities.

333
00:30:14,780 --> 00:30:22,600
Like many other practical forms of technology, America's first weather satellite emerged from a military project.

334
00:30:23,880 --> 00:30:36,260
Tyros-1, which was launched April 1, 1960, grew out of a study that one of the companies had made about the possible use for reconnaissance satellite.

335
00:30:36,700 --> 00:30:40,720
It had a television camera and produced images of clouds.

336
00:30:40,721 --> 00:30:47,720
The images Tyros-1 beamed back to Earth would never have sufficed for military reconnaissance,

337
00:30:47,721 --> 00:30:56,720
but the ability to see cloud formations over large regions of the Earth had an immediate impact on weather forecasting and storm warnings.

338
00:30:56,721 --> 00:31:03,720
In fact, Tyros-3, launched just four years after Sputnik in 1961,

339
00:31:03,721 --> 00:31:11,920
identified Hurricane Esther forming in the Atlantic Ocean more than two days before any other means of observation.

340
00:31:12,540 --> 00:31:18,780
If there's a hurricane bearing down on you, or there's a severe storm situation developing in the Midwest

341
00:31:18,781 --> 00:31:24,380
where tornadoes are going to be generated, the ability to give warnings that this is going to happen

342
00:31:24,381 --> 00:31:27,220
is the first step in saving lives.

343
00:31:27,800 --> 00:31:31,080
That's where the weather satellite comes in, particularly strong.

344
00:31:31,081 --> 00:31:38,080
Weather satellites designed to take high-resolution photos and gather precise atmospheric data

345
00:31:38,081 --> 00:31:43,880
generally operate in low-Earth polar orbits, about 200 to 400 miles high.

346
00:31:44,480 --> 00:31:51,060
However, the cloud pictures covering large regions of the Earth that most people are familiar with from weather forecasts

347
00:31:51,061 --> 00:31:56,800
are taken from geostationary orbit over 22,000 miles away.

348
00:31:56,801 --> 00:32:01,600
The same orbit Arthur C. Clarke advocated in 1945.

349
00:32:03,040 --> 00:32:11,580
At this distance, the satellite rotates at the same speed as the Earth, continuously remaining over the same spot on the globe.

350
00:32:13,420 --> 00:32:20,680
Before the 1960s, reconnaissance aircraft often couldn't spot hurricanes forming far out in the Atlantic

351
00:32:20,681 --> 00:32:26,640
until they were hours away from slamming into Caribbean islands or the southeastern United States.

352
00:32:26,800 --> 00:32:35,020
Thanks to the global coverage of weather satellites, people now have days to evacuate.

353
00:32:35,021 --> 00:32:41,420
And fewer doubters stay behind to ride it out, since they can see the huge swirling images

354
00:32:41,421 --> 00:32:49,080
and hear the accurate data about wind speeds and rainfall amounts on their own TV screens.

355
00:32:49,081 --> 00:32:54,580
Today's weather satellites are also capable of much more than taking pictures of clouds.

356
00:32:54,581 --> 00:33:00,260
They provide high-resolution images for extremely accurate three-dimensional maps and gather

357
00:33:00,261 --> 00:33:06,260
incredibly precise data about global weather systems and the upper atmosphere.

358
00:33:06,261 --> 00:33:11,620
They are even equipped with a search and rescue feature for people traveling in remote parts

359
00:33:11,621 --> 00:33:14,020
of the world.

360
00:33:14,021 --> 00:33:20,520
In addition to the most common use, which is for boats, it's used for downed aircraft,

361
00:33:20,521 --> 00:33:25,300
where if they are in serious trouble, they can activate the radio transmitter,

362
00:33:25,540 --> 00:33:30,320
and it sends out a radio transmission that is received by the satellite,

363
00:33:30,321 --> 00:33:37,700
and that then is relayed to Coast Guard or whoever to launch a search and try to rescue them.

364
00:33:38,040 --> 00:33:40,140
And it has been extremely successful.

365
00:33:40,380 --> 00:33:43,500
They measure the number of saves in hundreds.

366
00:33:45,320 --> 00:33:50,420
Besides weather satellites, Earth Resources orbiters are also helping researchers

367
00:33:50,421 --> 00:33:52,505
learn more about our planet.

368
00:33:52,506 --> 00:33:57,420
The Landsat family of satellites take a variety of high-resolution

369
00:33:57,540 --> 00:34:04,540
images which can be transformed by computers into 3D animations to assess crop yields,

370
00:34:04,541 --> 00:34:10,660
identify fault lines, aid in urban planning, and manage other natural resources such as

371
00:34:10,661 --> 00:34:11,361
forests.

372
00:34:11,362 --> 00:34:17,660
Because satellites can provide accurate positioning information, a growing number of

373
00:34:17,661 --> 00:34:23,660
A growing number of consumer applications are taking advantage of this technology.

374
00:34:23,661 --> 00:34:34,660
The Global Positioning System, or GPS, uses four or more satellites to fix a transmitter's position on the Earth to within a few yards.

375
00:34:34,661 --> 00:34:40,660
Such accuracy allows small boats and rental cars to navigate by satellite.

376
00:34:40,661 --> 00:34:47,600
We could pull up a screen in a car and see exactly where we're located and plot the course to where we need to go.

377
00:34:47,700 --> 00:34:52,260
I don't know about you, but I get lost all the time, and as my wife always says, I don't want to ask for directions.

378
00:34:52,740 --> 00:34:57,220
This enables me to do it without asking anybody else except a satellite.

379
00:34:57,880 --> 00:35:05,600
While man-made orbiters fulfill a variety of functions such as weather forecasting, navigation, or military reconnaissance,

380
00:35:05,601 --> 00:35:16,360
They are all, in a sense, communication satellites, because if they cannot send images or data back to Earth, then they are simply pieces of space junk.

381
00:35:18,240 --> 00:35:26,740
Coming up, from a crude, aluminized balloon to high-tech machines that can handle 120,000 phone calls simultaneously,

382
00:35:27,240 --> 00:35:33,640
the extraordinary evolution of communication satellites has rapidly changed our world.

383
00:35:35,600 --> 00:35:48,340
Like scientific, military and weather satellites, communication satellites also evolved from

384
00:35:48,341 --> 00:35:50,180
humble beginnings.

385
00:35:50,181 --> 00:35:56,440
In fact, the first communication satellite, Echo-1, launched in 1960, was nothing more

386
00:35:56,441 --> 00:35:59,660
than a 100-foot wide aluminized balloon.

387
00:35:59,661 --> 00:36:06,820
It was a passive satellite that one could point a beam at and bounce a radio beacon

388
00:36:06,821 --> 00:36:09,040
off of to someplace else.

389
00:36:09,041 --> 00:36:12,660
You could actually see this thing in orbit as it flew over because it was so large and

390
00:36:12,661 --> 00:36:14,280
it reflected so nicely.

391
00:36:14,281 --> 00:36:18,780
President Eisenhower listened to a test of his own voice being beamed across the country.

392
00:36:18,781 --> 00:36:25,620
In this first experiment in communications involving the use of a satellite balloon known

393
00:36:25,621 --> 00:36:32,620
as echo. This is one more significant step in the United States program of space...

394
00:36:32,621 --> 00:36:38,620
However, most people realized this was hardly the most effective means of communication.

395
00:36:38,621 --> 00:36:44,687
In 1962, AT&T developed Telstar, the world's first commercial satellite.

396
00:36:44,688 --> 00:36:46,120
It could actively

397
00:36:46,121 --> 00:36:51,731
receive and retransmit signals. But it operated in a low Earth orbit.

398
00:36:51,732 --> 00:36:53,520
This meant that it had

399
00:36:53,521 --> 00:36:57,280
to be tracked by moving satellite dishes on the ground.

400
00:36:57,281 --> 00:37:03,080
If any problem occurred during tracking, the communication link would be lost.

401
00:37:03,081 --> 00:37:08,360
In addition, Telstar was only available over the United States for a few minutes at a time

402
00:37:08,361 --> 00:37:13,300
before quickly circling the rest of the globe.

403
00:37:13,301 --> 00:37:21,320
Then in 1963, the Hughes Corporation launched the much smaller 150-pound Syncom-1, the world's

404
00:37:21,321 --> 00:37:24,901
first geostationary communication satellite.

405
00:37:24,902 --> 00:37:28,320
It could carry only 240 telephone calls at

406
00:37:28,321 --> 00:37:35,060
a time, but since Syncom was over 22,000 miles above the equator, it rotated at the same

407
00:37:35,061 --> 00:37:40,760
rate as the Earth, which eliminated both the need for tracking and the problem of intermittent

408
00:37:40,761 --> 00:37:41,840
availability.

409
00:37:41,841 --> 00:37:48,440
Even though Syncom was a relatively lightweight satellite by today's times and used relatively

410
00:37:48,441 --> 00:37:51,928
simple technology, it did enable John F.

411
00:37:51,929 --> 00:37:56,200
Kennedy to make a telephone call from Washington,

412
00:37:56,201 --> 00:38:01,060
D.C. to a ship off the coast of Africa.

413
00:38:01,061 --> 00:38:03,818
This first test proved that Arthur C.

414
00:38:03,819 --> 00:38:08,440
Clarke's visionary concept from 1945 worked, and geostationary

415
00:38:08,441 --> 00:38:13,060
orbit became the standard location for most communications satellites.

416
00:38:13,061 --> 00:38:21,180
Today, more than 150 of these modern marvels operate in this position, serving almost every country on Earth.

417
00:38:23,280 --> 00:38:27,640
These pioneering efforts made the formation of Intelsat possible.

418
00:38:27,641 --> 00:38:37,700
This is an international organization originally advocated by President Kennedy, designed to link all the nations of the world by satellite communications.

419
00:38:37,701 --> 00:38:44,700
Intelsat 1, also built by Hughes, was launched in 1965.

420
00:38:44,701 --> 00:38:55,700
The effort was a great success, and today there are over 20 Intelsat orbiters bringing our world together.

421
00:38:55,701 --> 00:39:03,700
Modern communications satellites are made with some of the lightest and strongest materials on Earth.

422
00:39:03,701 --> 00:39:04,401
on Earth.

423
00:39:04,402 --> 00:39:09,180
The graphite epoxy material that we're using in our satellites to keep the weight down

424
00:39:09,181 --> 00:39:14,245
is about half the weight of aluminum and 10 times the strength.

425
00:39:14,246 --> 00:39:17,140
In order to keep liquid fuel weights

426
00:39:17,141 --> 00:39:23,544
down, solar panels are used to power most of the onboard electronic components.

427
00:39:23,545 --> 00:39:24,760
Usually smaller

428
00:39:24,761 --> 00:39:31,433
satellites requiring less solar power are built in a drum shape about 21 feet tall.

429
00:39:31,434 --> 00:39:32,800
Larger satellites

430
00:39:32,801 --> 00:39:36,800
use expansive solar wings which are unfolded in orbit,

431
00:39:36,801 --> 00:39:39,800
leading to widths of more than 85 feet.

432
00:39:48,800 --> 00:39:51,800
Satellites have also dramatically affected our culture

433
00:39:51,801 --> 00:39:54,800
by relaying television signals around the globe.

434
00:39:54,801 --> 00:39:58,800
The 1964 Olympics were in Japan.

435
00:39:58,801 --> 00:40:09,400
And for the first time using CINCOM, we were able to see the Olympics in the United States transmitted live from Japan.

436
00:40:10,200 --> 00:40:18,200
And that brought home, loud and clear, how important satellite communications could be for television distribution.

437
00:40:19,300 --> 00:40:27,320
Satellite television transmissions in the 1980s allowed the people of the Soviet Union to see how the Western world lived.

438
00:40:27,321 --> 00:40:34,560
And today, Chinese citizens also have increasing access to news and information from other parts of the world.

439
00:40:35,560 --> 00:40:37,440
The world will never be the same.

440
00:40:38,280 --> 00:40:42,660
There is no longer any remote places in this world because of satellite communications.

441
00:40:43,880 --> 00:40:48,900
Thanks to stronger amplification of television signals by today's satellites,

442
00:40:49,500 --> 00:40:54,660
consumers can now receive broadcasts directly on 18-inch wide dishes.

443
00:40:54,661 --> 00:41:01,660
As improvements continue, these satellite receivers may become an integral part of most households,

444
00:41:01,661 --> 00:41:08,660
providing 400 channels of incoming television and data, as well as one channel of outgoing video.

445
00:41:08,661 --> 00:41:15,660
This outgoing channel will make video conferences as easy as phone calls are today.

446
00:41:15,661 --> 00:41:23,660
Communication satellites have transformed our world into a global village in less than four decades.

447
00:41:23,661 --> 00:41:29,660
Today we can pick up the phone and instantly talk to someone else almost anywhere on the planet.

448
00:41:29,661 --> 00:41:36,660
Or turn on our televisions and watch foreign and domestic events unfold before our eyes.

449
00:41:36,661 --> 00:41:43,660
International faxing, high-speed modems and email using phone lines and satellites

450
00:41:43,661 --> 00:41:52,660
also allow businesses, researchers and ordinary people to transmit data around the globe in a matter of seconds.

451
00:41:53,660 --> 00:41:59,040
Real-time, worldwide communications was something that didn't exist before the 1960s

452
00:41:59,041 --> 00:42:00,740
in the communications satellite revolution.

453
00:42:00,900 --> 00:42:02,620
It has changed our whole way of life.

454
00:42:03,060 --> 00:42:07,080
Our whole society, our economy, is built upon those capabilities.

455
00:42:13,180 --> 00:42:20,220
When American explorer and discoverer satellites were racing against the Soviet Union 40 years ago,

456
00:42:20,221 --> 00:42:25,220
No one ever imagined that the space above our planet might become overcrowded.

457
00:42:25,221 --> 00:42:35,220
Yet this is a growing concern since objects in geostationary orbit need to be positioned a certain distance from the Earth and over the equator.

458
00:42:35,221 --> 00:42:42,220
They also need to be located about 1,000 miles apart from each other, so their signals don't get mixed.

459
00:42:42,221 --> 00:42:49,220
Therefore, there are only a certain number of slots left to be occupied, an issue that the United Nations is looking into.

460
00:42:49,221 --> 00:42:49,921
into.

461
00:42:49,922 --> 00:42:56,220
I believe that with good intelligent planning and good coordination by the International

462
00:42:56,460 --> 00:43:03,460
Telecommunications Union, there will be enough slots for satellites well into the 21st century.

463
00:43:05,680 --> 00:43:12,680
Another concern as space becomes more crowded is orbital debris, also known as space junk.

464
00:43:12,900 --> 00:43:18,560
This consists of various size objects such as portions of expended rocket stages, small

465
00:43:18,561 --> 00:43:24,380
parts that have come off of spacecraft, and even paint chips.

466
00:43:24,381 --> 00:43:30,060
Each dot on this computer animation represents an actual piece of space debris.

467
00:43:30,061 --> 00:43:36,160
Many of these items do eventually burn up in Earth's upper atmosphere, but this process

468
00:43:36,161 --> 00:43:42,880
can sometimes take decades to happen as their orbits gradually decay.

469
00:43:42,881 --> 00:43:47,960
On the surface, something as small as a paint chip may not seem to be much of a concern,

470
00:43:47,961 --> 00:43:54,580
But since it is traveling 17,500 miles an hour, it can still have a significant impact.

471
00:43:55,400 --> 00:44:01,540
In fact, a speck of paint from a satellite did hit one of the windows of the Challenger space shuttle in 1983,

472
00:44:02,360 --> 00:44:04,260
causing a half-centimeter pit.

473
00:44:06,520 --> 00:44:10,500
Obviously, a larger object could have disastrous results.

474
00:44:11,500 --> 00:44:17,380
It does exist. It's up there. It's not a major problem, at least at the present.

475
00:44:17,960 --> 00:44:21,960
I would be more concerned about meteorites and things of that nature.

476
00:44:23,180 --> 00:44:29,680
Despite these concerns, satellites are bringing our world together at a faster pace than any other form of technology.

477
00:44:30,700 --> 00:44:35,380
The term live via satellite used to denote a special world event.

478
00:44:36,140 --> 00:44:40,420
However, today the operation of satellites goes largely unnoticed.

479
00:44:40,421 --> 00:44:45,440
Not because they are unimportant, but because they are so important.

480
00:44:45,441 --> 00:44:52,200
Without the weather forecasting that they make possible, without the global communications

481
00:44:52,201 --> 00:44:58,200
that they make possible, without the exploration and mapping that they make possible, if you

482
00:44:58,201 --> 00:45:02,600
think what the world would be without those surfaces, that's when you begin to realize

483
00:45:02,601 --> 00:45:06,600
how important they are.

484
00:45:06,601 --> 00:45:11,160
Satellites are firmly embedded in so many of our modern technologies, and they function

485
00:45:11,161 --> 00:45:17,280
so consistently for years on end that we can enjoy the luxury of taking them for granted.

486
00:45:19,760 --> 00:45:23,740
The possibility for the future in satellites is almost limitless.

487
00:45:23,980 --> 00:45:26,860
Whatever we can conceive, we can probably do.

488
00:45:28,380 --> 00:45:33,820
Because of the visionary work of pioneers who stake their careers and their reputations

489
00:45:33,821 --> 00:45:39,980
on an unproven concept, satellites have transformed our world in just 40 years.

490
00:45:39,981 --> 00:45:44,600
from three-pound gadgets which looked more like grapefruits than spacecraft

491
00:45:44,601 --> 00:45:49,800
to incredibly powerful and complex machines weighing thousands of pounds.

492
00:45:51,500 --> 00:45:54,480
Considering the remarkably rapid evolution of satellites

493
00:45:54,481 --> 00:45:56,680
that we have witnessed in the past four decades,

494
00:45:56,681 --> 00:46:00,600
it seems that the only certainty for the next four decades

495
00:46:00,601 --> 00:46:04,120
is that satellites will continue to amaze us

496
00:46:04,121 --> 00:46:07,240
in ways that we cannot even imagine today.

